---
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: traefik
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# THE ROLLBACK LEVER. All seven slice-4 policies carry this same conditional, and this file
   carries the reasoning the other six point back to.

   `netpol_baseline_enforced` is the documented undo for slice 4, and it only works if each
   bespoke policy renders allow-all when the flag is false. `kubectl apply` never prunes, so a
   `when:` on the include in tasks/main.yml would undo nothing: the policies already applied
   stay live and keep fencing, minus the traefik/prometheus grants the flag also switches off --
   strictly worse than not rolling back at all. Real deletion is not available either;
   `kubectl delete networkpolicy` is deny-listed for agents and refused by the readonly SA.
   Slice 3 established this render-allow-all pattern for the observability policies
   (networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2); slice 4 follows it.

   `| bool` matches the `when:` in tasks/main.yml. Without it the two disagree for a STRING:
   `-e netpol_baseline_enforced=false` is truthy to Jinja (this branch renders, fencing
   applied) and False to Ansible's `when:` (every probe task skipped) -- enforcement with no
   probe, from the exact override an operator reaches for mid-incident. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_enforced | bool %}
    # The front door, in two rules because the two ports admit different sources.
    #
    # THE PORT NUMBERS HERE ARE POD PORTS, NOT SERVICE PORTS. A NetworkPolicy matches the
    # destination port on the POD, after kube-proxy has already DNAT'd the Service port to the
    # targetPort. traefik's Service maps 80 -> targetPort `http` and 443 -> `https`, and those
    # container ports are 8000 and 8443. Writing 80/443 here therefore admits traffic to two
    # ports nothing listens on, and denies every in-cluster caller of the real ones.
    # Measured 2026-08-20: that is exactly what happened -- the slice-1 probe's control leg
    # failed with "cannot reach traefik:80" while the same route answered 200 from the LAN,
    # because external traffic arrives over the VIP path rather than the ClusterIP.
    # Every other service in this slice maps its ports 1:1, which is why only this file was hit.
    #
    # :8000 stays open to every source. It is the http->https redirect entrypoint
    # (static-config.yaml.j2, entryPoint.to: ":443") -- every client that arrives on plain HTTP
    # hits that redirect before reaching :8443 -- and all four netpol probe control legs dial
    # traefik:80 to prove the probe itself has a network. (uptime-kuma's monitors do NOT depend
    # on :80: they're https:// and so traverse :8443, or for the Pi services dial daniel-pi
    # directly, bypassing traefik.) Nothing on :8000 reaches a backend, so an origin-direct
    # client gets a redirect to a port the next rule fences.
    - ports:
        - port: 8000
          protocol: TCP
    # :8443 admits only netpol_baseline_traefik_https_sources (defaults/main.yml): Cloudflare's
    # published ranges, the LAN, the WireGuard client range, and the pod CIDR. Until 2026-09-18
    # this port was open to every source, with the reasoning that an ipBlock list "would have to
    # enumerate the internet". That was the wrong frame (#1974): the only source that is
    # supposed to reach a public `Host(x.<domain>)` Rule is Cloudflare's edge, and its ranges are
    # already enumerated in `cloudflare_ips` for forwardedHeaders.trustedIPs. An origin-direct
    # client -- anyone who resolves the origin IP and sends the public Host header -- skipped
    # Cloudflare's WAF and bot rules, arrived with no trusted X-Forwarded-For, and so shared ONE
    # rate-limit bucket keyed on "" with every other origin-direct request. This list is the
    # origin lock the Docker-era DOCKER-USER chain used to be. Cloudflare Tunnel and a
    # ClientIP() clause on the public Rule were considered and rejected in #1974; Authenticated
    # Origin Pulls is #1990, on top of this.
    #
    # ETP=Local preserves the source address on the VIP path, so each ipBlock matches what the
    # client really is. The LAN range covers the WireGuard clients as well: wg-easy on daniel-pi
    # runs its default PostUp, which masquerades `wg_client_subnet` to the Pi's LAN address
    # (docker-compose.yml.j2 sets no WG_POST_UP); the wg_client_subnet entry is the un-NAT'd
    # shape, kept so a PostUp change does not lock WireGuard out.
    #
    # The pod CIDR here is the WHOLE /16 on purpose, which docs/networkpolicy-default-deny.md
    # ("Correction carried into this design") forbids for the baseline. There a /16 readmits
    # every pod to a fenced workload and cancels the default-deny. Here every pod is a legitimate
    # caller: traefik is the ingress, and an in-cluster caller of a `.local.` route (the Kuma
    # prober, monitor-bridge, homepage's widgets) reaches the VIP as its own 10.42.x.y address.
    # This port admitted every pod before this rule existed, so in-cluster reachability is
    # unchanged and the rule is a strict narrowing. Both cni0 gateways and both flannel.1
    # addresses sit inside the /16, so host-originated callers (the three host crons that dial
    # traefik, a cross-node hop) need no separate /32 grant.
    #
    # Seven of the Cloudflare entries are IPv6. kube-router buckets ipBlocks per address family
    # (evalIPBlockPeer in pkg/controllers/netpol/policy.go, v2.6) and installs an ipset only for
    # a family the node runs, so on this single-stack v4 cluster the v6 entries are inert rather
    # than a broken ipset. They stay so the list equals `cloudflare_ips` exactly, which is what
    # cloudflare-ip-drift.sh compares against upstream.
    - from:
{% for cidr in netpol_baseline_traefik_https_sources %}
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ cidr }}
{% endfor %}
      ports:
        - port: 8443
          protocol: TCP
    # :8080 is the port worth fencing. prometheus moved to the observability namespace in
    # slice 3, so this is cross-namespace: namespaceSelector and podSelector are siblings in
    # ONE from item, which ANDs them. As separate items they would OR, admitting every pod in
    # observability and every prometheus anywhere.
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: observability
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: prometheus
      ports:
        - port: 8080
          protocol: TCP
        # 6060 is the crowdsec-agent SIDECAR's metrics port, admitted to prometheus alone in
        # the same from item (#1694). Without it the `crowdsec-traefik-agent` scrape job in
        # claude-otel/templates/prometheus.yaml.j2 reads `up == 0` forever — the grant and
        # that job only work as a pair. Nothing else may reach it: agent metrics enumerate
        # every parser and scenario the edge runs, which is reconnaissance for an attacker.
        - port: 6060
          protocol: TCP
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
